WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.120
No story is complete without a good kiss —&nbsp;
and a kiss done right, at the perfect moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.840
can mark the definitive moment in a movie.&nbsp;
That goes doubly for last kisses, too. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.920
they're long or short, bittersweet or devastating,&nbsp;
these are the best last kisses in movie history.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.640
Inevitably, many last kisses in the movies&nbsp;
tend to be tinged with a touch of tragedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:24.720
and Ridley Scott's Gladiator takes that&nbsp;
rule to its heart-wrenching extreme.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.040
In Gladiator, Hispano-Roman soldier&nbsp;
Maximus faithfully serves the Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.920
Emperor Marcus Aurelius, who selects him&nbsp;
to be his heir over his own son, Commodus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.000
Romans being Romans, Commodus decides&nbsp;
to kill his father before the decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.040
becomes official. When Maximus refuses to serve&nbsp;
the new Emperor, Commodus has him arrested.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.080
Maximus soon escapes, however, and rides home&nbsp;
to his wife and son — only to find that they and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.880
everyone else on their farm have been killed by&nbsp;
Commodus' men. It's here that Maximus tearfully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:58.240
kisses his wife's scorched feet before collapsing&nbsp;
in despair. He's then kidnapped by a slave trader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.040
and, well, the whole gladiator&nbsp;
part of the movie happens.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.240
Compounded by Hans Zimmer and&nbsp;
Lisa Gerrard's powerful score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.040
this scene provides a rare glimpse of emotion in&nbsp;
a movie better known for its epic fight sequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.080
It reminds the audience that, behind Maximus'&nbsp;
soldierly exterior, he's just a man who loves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.240
his wife and son. It's also the moment&nbsp;
that sets up Gladiator's iconic ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.440
in which a victorious Maximus finally&nbsp;
reunites with his family in the afterlife.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.920
Everybody knew going in that Titanic was never&nbsp;
going to end well. Nonetheless, you still can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.200
help but get emotionally invested in the romance&nbsp;
between society girl Rose and broke artist Jack.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.600
Jack and Rose's most famous kiss, of course,&nbsp;
is their full-blown make-out session on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.600
ship's bow, long before things go&nbsp;
wrong. But it's not their last. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.480
figuring out which of their kisses is technically&nbsp;
their last is actually a little tricky. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.760
Jack and Rose don't have a lot of time for PDAs&nbsp;
as they're trying to escape a sinking ship. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:55.520
however, after Jack perishes in&nbsp;
the icy waters of the Atlantic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.400
Rose kisses his frozen hands in a final&nbsp;
goodbye — and promises to never let go.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.560
"I'll never let go. I promise."

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.040
Rose then immediately lets go. She does hold&nbsp;
onto his memory, though, which, in hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.840
is probably what she meant. During the movie's&nbsp;
final scene, Jack and Rose reunite at last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.760
on the Titanic's first-class staircase.&nbsp;
They're surrounded by other passengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.400
who cheer when the couple kisses one last time.&nbsp;
Is this a dream? Is Rose in the afterlife?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.840
The meaning of the scene is deliberately&nbsp;
vague, but watching these two characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.720
reunite after 83 years with a kiss for the&nbsp;
ages will thaw even the iciest of hearts.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.480
Not all last kisses happen between lovers. Take&nbsp;
Thelma &amp; Louise, for example. In this movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.280
best friends Thelma and Louise leave the former's&nbsp;
controlling husband behind for a fun weekend on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.920
the road. Instead, they end up killing a man who&nbsp;
attacks Thelma, going on the run, picking up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.880
drifter, robbing a convenience store, and blowing&nbsp;
up a truck. But the best final kiss in this movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.000
isn't between Thelma and her new drifter lover&nbsp;
J.D., or between Louise and her on/off boyfriend.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.160
Parked by the Grand Canyon with a motorcade of&nbsp;
cops behind them, the two friends-on-the-run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.000
decide to go out with a bang — and they&nbsp;
mark their final moments with a kiss.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.200
Unlike many movie kisses between women, this&nbsp;
kiss is neither salacious nor played to titillate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.640
straight male audience members. It's just a moment&nbsp;
of true love between two friends. In the 20th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.440
anniversary edition Blu-ray commentary, Susan&nbsp;
Sarandon took credit for this moment, saying:

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.000
"I had mentioned to Geena&nbsp;
that I was going to kiss her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.640
but I don't think Ridley&nbsp;
[Scott, the director] knew."

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.640
Fortunately, Sarandon pulled it off —&nbsp;
and a moment of movie history was born.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:34.240
In Moulin Rouge! the tragedy of the last kiss&nbsp;
between tragically impoverished writer Christian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.880
and aspiring actress Satine is that it comes&nbsp;
in the wake of a major victory for the couple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.160
The pair have finally ousted Satine's controlling&nbsp;
patron, the Duke, and delivered their vibrant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.720
wild, Bohemian musical to La Belle Époque&nbsp;
Paris. But thanks to an opening scene featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.520
a devastated Christian, the audience has known&nbsp;
all along that something is going to go wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.040
And by the time the movie's grand&nbsp;
musical finale comes to a close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.120
Satine is also well aware that she is&nbsp;
suffering from a fatal bout of consumption.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:10.320
"I'm cold, I'm cold, I'm cold… Hold me."

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.480
As Christian slowly realizes what's&nbsp;
happening, he holds her, kissing her softly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.720
as she begs him to tell their story. Their&nbsp;
final scene brings together the fantastical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.200
Moulin Rouge! stylization with a sense&nbsp;
of heartbreakingly authentic human drama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.160
As writer and director Baz&nbsp;
Luhrmann told The Guardian:

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:30.720
"[It's about] the transition of youthful idealism&nbsp;
to when you realize that there are things bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.440
than you — people die, some relationships&nbsp;
cannot be — and you are destroyed by that."

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.000
Theatre fans will tell you that William&nbsp;
Shakespeare wrote the ultimate final kiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.680
back in the late 16th century.&nbsp;
But some 400 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.160
master of stylistic drama Baz Luhrmann&nbsp;
decided to put a new spin on Shakespeare's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.720
version with his film production of Romeo +&nbsp;
Juliet. The Shakespearean dialogue remained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.520
but the swords became guns, the costumes&nbsp;
were updated, and a fish tank, of all things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.880
played a prominent part in the iconic couple's&nbsp;
meet cute. Shakespeare suddenly became '90s cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.840
thanks in large part to Leonardo DiCaprio and&nbsp;
Claire Danes, who breathed life into the iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.640
star-crossed lovers... and then, of course, killed&nbsp;
them both off in the most brutal way possible.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.640
The CliffNotes version of the ending is&nbsp;
that Romeo, believing that Juliet is dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.200
breaks into her tomb, intending to kill&nbsp;
himself. Juliet, who is only sedated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.840
wakes up just as he downs a deadly poison.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:32.320
"I'll kiss thy lips. Haply some poison&nbsp;
yet doth hang on them." and the kiss

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:37.200
Juliet then turns Romeo's gun on herself. This&nbsp;
morbid moment turns the hallmark of young love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.520
into a deadly weapon, just as the couple's&nbsp;
forbidden love ultimately led to their doom.

00:05:43.025 --> 00:05:45.440
Shakespeare in Love takes more&nbsp;
than a few liberties with history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.800
and doesn't shy away from playing with&nbsp;
some tongue-in-cheek humor. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:52.080
the love story at the heart of it&nbsp;
all feels absolutely authentic.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.600
The wealthy Viola is prevented from acting on the&nbsp;
London stage by both her social status and gender.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.600
So she dresses up as a man and lands&nbsp;
the starring role in a new play called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.560
Ethel the Pirate's Daughter by some mediocre&nbsp;
playwright named Will Shakespeare. Shakespeare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.560
soon falls in love with Viola, and the&nbsp;
two begin an affair after Shakespeare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.680
figures out that his leading actor&nbsp;
and his crush are the same person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:18.080
He even starts writing the play that becomes&nbsp;
Romeo and Juliet, based on their relationship.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:23.280
But these star-crossed lovers are doomed from&nbsp;
the start. Viola is engaged to evil Lord Wessex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.360
and they're planning on moving to Virginia. Even&nbsp;
though they marry on the morning of the play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.120
Viola rushes back to the theater.&nbsp;
Through various circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.760
Shakespeare and Viola end up playing Romeo&nbsp;
and Juliet, respectively. But when the play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.560
ends — to thunderous applause — they have to&nbsp;
say goodbye. Viola kisses Will one more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.280
then leaves without looking back. It's a&nbsp;
devastating, unavoidable goodbye in which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.840
two lovers leading the story don't get what they&nbsp;
want — which seems apt, considering the context.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.200
For such an epic love story, Cold Mountain doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.880
have many kissing scenes — but it does&nbsp;
make the few that happen really count.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.760
Ada is a fancy Charleston lady who falls&nbsp;
for silent and sturdy country boy Inman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.600
when she and her father move to Cold Mountain,&nbsp;
North Carolina. In 1861, Inman goes to fight for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.000
the Confederacy in the Civil War. The two kiss&nbsp;
passionately before he leaves, then spend the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.400
next three years apart, only able to communicate&nbsp;
with each other through the letters they write.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.880
After a ferocious battle, Inman abandons&nbsp;
the army and sets out on the long journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.720
home. The two are eventually, uh,&nbsp;
"reacquainted", but the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.840
Inman gets into a shootout with a Home Guardsman&nbsp;
who's been pursuing Ada. He kills the man but is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:34.880
fatally wounded himself. Ada holds him as he&nbsp;
dies, kissing his forehead and comforting him.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.280
By the time you get to Inman and Ada's last kiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.360
you've seen the both of them go through so&nbsp;
much to reunite that it feels blisteringly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.360
unfair that they don't get a happy ending.&nbsp;
Sadly, though, their ill-fated romance tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.840
the same story of countless lovers who have&nbsp;
been doomed by wartime and human cruelty.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.720
Not many teens have a ghost as a best friend, but&nbsp;
in 1995's Casper, Kat is used to the supernatural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:01.280
thanks to her dad's paranormal investigations.&nbsp;
As a result, she meets the ghost Casper when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:05.360
they move into his ancestral home, Whipstaff&nbsp;
Manor. Although their initial meeting is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.640
little awkward, Casper wins over Kat and her dad,&nbsp;
James. But there's more to it than that — Casper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:15.600
has had a crush on Kat since even before they&nbsp;
met, having seen a news report about James' work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:19.920
Sadly, the whole being dead thing throws a&nbsp;
significant wrench in their would-be romance.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.320
However, in the basement, Kat and Casper&nbsp;
conveniently discover the Lazarus machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.640
which Casper's father built to bring his son&nbsp;
back to life. But there's only enough formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.920
for one ghost-to-human transformation, and when&nbsp;
James dies in an accident, Casper sacrifices his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.960
shot at resurrection so that Kat doesn't have to&nbsp;
lose her dad. As thanks, Kat's mom returns from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:43.440
the afterlife and makes Casper human... but&nbsp;
only for the night of Kat's Halloween party.&nbsp;

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.720
Casper dances with Kat, and they kiss, right as&nbsp;
he turns back into his ghost form. It's sweet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.480
it's sad, and it's also a little funny.&nbsp;
What more could you ask of a final kiss?

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:58.320
The sexiest kiss in 1990's Ghost obviously&nbsp;
happens during that famous pottery scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.040
But the last kiss between Demi Moore's Molly&nbsp;
and Patrick Swayze's Sam is by far the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.200
emotionally resonant of the movie — and&nbsp;
may be one of the all-time greats, to boot.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.680
After being killed by a mugger, Sam becomes a&nbsp;
ghost. You know, hence the name of the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.360
While he can see his girlfriend Molly,&nbsp;
she doesn't know he's there. Sam then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.160
stumbles across a supposedly fake psychic,&nbsp;
Oda Mae, who can actually hear and see him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.560
Oda Mae tries to convince Molly that she's&nbsp;
communicating with Sam, but a grief-stricken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:29.280
Molly is skeptical. Meanwhile, Sam learns to&nbsp;
move physical objects with his ghostly energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.440
and eventually, he rescues Oda Mae and&nbsp;
Molly from the man who had him killed.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.560
Suddenly, Molly can see and hear Sam, too. He&nbsp;
realizes that, now that he's saved her and avenged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:43.200
his own death, he has to move to the afterlife.&nbsp;
So Sam and Molly say goodbye with a kiss.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:50.080
"It's amazing, Molly. The love&nbsp;
inside… you take it with you."

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.120
Swayze and Moore nail the bittersweetness of this&nbsp;
final kiss. In fact, it was Swayze's audition for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.920
this scene that convinced the previously skeptical&nbsp;
filmmakers to cast him, and Moore perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:05.680
balances her character's frankly understandable&nbsp;
sense of confusion, joy, and sadness. Altogether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.920
it just works. There's a reason this was the&nbsp;
biggest movie of the year in 1990, you know.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.680
If there is a Movie Kisses Hall of&nbsp;
Fame — and there should be — The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:17.920
Notebook definitely has a place in it,&nbsp;
thanks entirely to one particular scene.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:22.480
Lumber mill worker Noah and heiress Allie&nbsp;
defy her family's attempts to tear them apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:26.800
and triumphantly reunite with a passionate&nbsp;
kiss in the middle of a summer rainstorm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:30.000
That moment won Ryan Gosling&nbsp;
and Rachel McAdams the coveted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.680
MTV Movie Award for Best Kiss in 2005 and&nbsp;
immediately went down in cinema history.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.920
But it's Noah and Allie's final kiss that's&nbsp;
the most powerful. Having grown old together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:44.160
they both come down with various illnesses&nbsp;
and are taken to the same hospital. Allie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.080
who can barely remember Noah, is in the&nbsp;
dementia ward, while Noah is in for a heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:52.160
attack. But Noah sneaks into Allie's&nbsp;
room, and she briefly recognizes him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:56.080
After the two decide that they want to pass&nbsp;
away together, they kiss one last time.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.720
The kiss in the rain has all the&nbsp;
passion of a long-delayed young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:02.960
romance. But the final kiss contains the&nbsp;
true love and understanding of a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:06.560
who really lived up to their promise to love&nbsp;
each other until the end, no matter what.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:13.360
James Bond is lucky in lust but never in love.&nbsp;
In 2006's Casino Royale, however, it looked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:18.560
the playboy spy had finally met his match, both&nbsp;
romantically and professionally, in Vesper Lynd.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:23.440
"So as charming as you are, Mr. Bond, I will&nbsp;
be keeping my eye on our government's money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:25.360
and off your perfectly formed arse."

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:26.640
"You noticed."

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:32.320
The two make it through a dangerous plot involving&nbsp;
shady organizations, creepy villains, and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:37.520
eye-watering torture scene, and are rewarded for&nbsp;
it with a romantic vacation in Venice. Bond even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.480
plans to resign from MI6... until he learns that&nbsp;
Vesper has betrayed him, having stolen the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:47.120
Bond won in a high-stakes poker game on behalf&nbsp;
of the people holding her former lover hostage.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:50.560
When the deal goes bad, Vesper&nbsp;
is taken captive by bad guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.120
and Bond manages to take them out, one-by-one.&nbsp;
But as the building they're in starts to sink,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:00.320
Vesper locks herself in an elevator, and refuses&nbsp;
to let Bond save her. He's eventually able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.200
get her out and back to the surface, but his&nbsp;
kiss of life quickly turns into a kiss goodbye.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.360
Now, Bond movies have had their fair share of&nbsp;
last kisses over the years, but this one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:14.160
particularly special. Bond's farewell to the one&nbsp;
woman he truly loved resonates well beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:18.720
end of the movie, and sets up the character's arc&nbsp;
for the rest of Daniel Craig's time in the role.

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:24.000
Otherwise known as Wonder Woman, Diana Prince&nbsp;
has had no experience kissing human men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:28.400
when she and US pilot Steve Trevor first&nbsp;
meet. But the unlikely pair still pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:32.080
off a truly romantic final kiss —&nbsp;
before hero duty calls, of course.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.840
After Wonder Woman has marched&nbsp;
through the No Man's Land of 1917&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.840
and liberated the village of Veld, a&nbsp;
well-deserved celebration is in order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.960
So Steve offers to teach her&nbsp;
how to dance like humans do.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:43.920
"You're awfully close."

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.560
"That's what it's all about."

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:54.400
But although sparks fly, neither Steve nor Diana&nbsp;
can imagine living an ordinary life together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.080
They go to a hotel room in the village&nbsp;
and nervously kiss... and that's all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:02.480
audience gets to see. Sadly for Diana, the&nbsp;
war gets between them, and Steve ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:06.400
leaving her in the middle of a battle — with&nbsp;
a declaration of love, but no goodbye kiss.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:10.160
If you're the kind of person who&nbsp;
gets a kick out of watching the slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.720
painful disintegration of a once-hopeful romance&nbsp;
that has long become tortured and twisted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.200
then boy is Blue Valentine the movie for you.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.960
Cindy and Dean fall in love at second&nbsp;
sight when she's a pre-med student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:25.760
and he's delivering furniture. When Cindy finds&nbsp;
out she's pregnant with her ex-boyfriend's child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.640
she and Dean get married. Five years later, their&nbsp;
relationship is cracking under his drinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:36.000
their money worries, and her despondency. Despite&nbsp;
their best attempts at salvaging the relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.760
things only get worse from there.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:42.080
Eventually, Cindy tells Dean they have to break&nbsp;
up, but he tries to convince her that they should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.960
stay together. He walks over to her and hugs her,&nbsp;
even as Cindy tenses up in his arms, and kisses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:51.040
her on the head. The physical contradiction&nbsp;
of his hug and her stiffness reflects the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.680
simple and tragic fact that they don't fit&nbsp;
together emotionally anymore. What was once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.680 --> 00:14:00.240
a gesture of love becomes a difficult goodbye,&nbsp;
and the scene's closing moments, intercut with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:04.560
footage from their wedding day, shows just how far&nbsp;
they've come from their hopeful, younger selves.

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:10.480
The lovers in 1945's Brief Encounter mostly&nbsp;
convey their electric chemistry through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:16.000
well, basically anything other than kissing. So&nbsp;
when they do finally smooch, sparks really sizzle.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:20.320
Laura is a bored housewife with a kind&nbsp;
but dull husband in 1940s England.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:23.840
Her weekly highlight is taking the&nbsp;
train to the nearby town of Milford.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:27.520
Waiting at the Milford station one&nbsp;
day, she meets a charming doctor, Alec,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:32.800
who's also married with kids. After accidentally&nbsp;
meeting again, they begin a covert relationship.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.800
Eventually, after a few near-misses, the&nbsp;
couple decide they can't continue their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:41.040
affair and spend one final day together. The&nbsp;
heart-wrenching twist is that they don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:45.360
get a private goodbye. At their last meeting,&nbsp;
they're interrupted by one of Laura's friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:49.600
Alec has to leave to catch his train, and all&nbsp;
he can do is squeeze Laura on the shoulder.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:54.000
The final kiss of the movie takes place earlier,&nbsp;
when they're also interrupted by the arrival of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:58.720
a friend. And even this was a brave move on the&nbsp;
filmmakers' part. Brief Encounter was considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.560
so scandalous because of its adultery&nbsp;
that it was initially banned in Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:06.960
While movies today are freer to show kisses&nbsp;
— even adulterous kisses — this one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:10.320
all the more powerful for being a daring&nbsp;
expression of rule-breaking passion.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:14.480
If there's one movie that best sums&nbsp;
up the burden of impossible love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.760
it's Casablanca. Grouchy American Rick Blaine is&nbsp;
keeping a low profile in Casablanca during World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:25.040
War II when his long-lost love, Ilsa Lund, walks&nbsp;
into his gin joint... accompanied by her husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:30.160
Czech Resistance leader Victor Lazlo. Rick hasn't&nbsp;
seen Ilsa since the previous summer, when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:34.720
fell in love in Paris, only for her to abandon&nbsp;
him suddenly. Now, she and Lazlo are trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:39.680
reach America to escape the Nazis, and Rick has&nbsp;
the exit papers they need in order to get out.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:44.880
Reunited and still very much in love, Ilsa and&nbsp;
Rick plan to use the papers to leave together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:48.640
But Rick has a change of heart, and&nbsp;
puts Lazlo and Ilsa on the plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:53.200
knowing that Lazlo needs Ilsa to continue his&nbsp;
important work. Many people think that Rick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:57.520
and Ilsa's last kiss happens at the airport, but&nbsp;
come on — that would have been kind of a giveaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.840
to her husband. The kiss actually happens at&nbsp;
Rick's apartment the night before, when Ilsa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:06.160
declares her love for Rick at long last. Ilsa and&nbsp;
Rick then release all that pent-up longing in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.640
perfectly-shot, dramatically soundtracked kiss&nbsp;
— which, inevitably, proves to be their last.

